+// Aura Glass: with the full-size-buffers key on, a blur texture never shrinks
+// and is not freed while its actor hides, for as long as the effect stays on
+// that actor (a detached effect is pooled, so it gives its buffers back).
+// Stock v77 shrinks a texture once its content has stayed below half for 2 s
+// and frees the kawase chain 5 s after opacity 0, so the next open of a menu,
+// an app folder or a banner reallocated the capture, the whole chain and the
+// shader target in its first frames. The size still starts at the content's
+// own and grows 1.25x at a time up to the largest monitor's: the first
+// version allocated that full size up front, about 60 MB per blurred actor
+// at 3440x1600 even for the 32 px top bar, some 700 MB over stock on this
+// desktop, and bought little. The chain and the shader target are sized from
+// the actor's unscaled clip, which a menu scaling in or a folder zooming from
+// its icon never changes; only the screen-space capture follows the scale,
+// and that shrinks from the actor's full size, so it fits. An actor pays one
+// reallocation the first time it outgrows its texture (a window's first
+// maximize), never on the next open.
+let fullSizeBuffers = false;
+
+export function set_full_size_buffers(value) {
+    fullSizeBuffers = value;
+}
+
+export function full_size_buffers() {
+    return fullSizeBuffers;
+}
+
+// Aura Glass: a Cogl texture gives its VRAM back only when GJS finalizes the
+// small JS object that wraps it, and GJS collects when the JS heap grows,
+// which these wrappers barely make it do. So a dropped blur buffer stayed
+// resident until some unrelated collection. On a 3440x1440 monitor with
+// full-size-buffers on, each overview open and close left 250-500 MB more
+// (the previews' clone captures and their reallocations), and GNOME Shell
+// went from 5.1 to 6.4 GB of an 8 GB card before a collection brought it
+// back (measured 2026-11-12). Every release adds its size here. Once 64 MiB
+// are waiting, one collection runs 3 s after the last release, at low
+// priority, so an animation that drops buffers frame after frame never has a
+// collection land inside it, and small menus coming and going never start one
+// on their own.
+const RELEASE_GC_BYTES = 64 * 1024 * 1024;
+const RELEASE_GC_DELAY_S = 3;
+let releasedBytes = 0;
+let releaseGcId = 0;
